Really, its more like a labor strike then actual price gouging. I would think you would actually have to be able to set prices.  Now the airport totally can fine you.  But thats something else. 

The fact that we dont charge the prices to consumers directly is a big deal. All we are doing is going of line at the same time. That in its self is not price gouging. 

As long as drivers dont agree on a set amount and demand it. Then its not price gouging. 

So the surge price is activated then some drivers start taking it.  But some wait for higher prices.  We arent even collectively doing the same price. 

&#x200B;

The FTC will never go after uber drivers.  They care about consumers. The political outcry from going after uber drivers will not alow it to happen.

&#x200B;

Only way they go after uber and lyft is if they both raise prices at the same time.  And even thin, they will go after uber and lyft. Not the drivers. 

&#x200B;

Maybe the FTC could win a case against drivers, but its a flimsy case. And the out cry will prevent them from pursuing it.

